		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=28263#p28263"><![CDATA[
Erin Catto's demonstration uses velocity-based constraints; I usually think of Lagrange multipliers as used with acceleration-based constraints, but I'm not sure of the exact definition and it could be the same thing. I think if you've modeled constraints, you've either used Lagrange multipliers without knowing it or something very similar.<br><br>As for using contact constraints, I think it certainly becomes necessary when you are using articulated bodies that collide with each other. Not everything is stacking boxes! (Although admittedly that <em class="text-italics">is</em> one decent test of a physics system.)<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1626">bone</a> — Mon Jul 09, 2012 4:23 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
